<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baquates</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Baquates were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> people of Roman Mauretania Tingitana, in present-day northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. Altars set up at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volubilis">Volubilis</a> between the second and fourth centuries CE record agreements and renewed treaties of peace between their chiefs and Roman governors, which show how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> managed the peoples beyond its frontier through diplomacy as well as force.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baquates.jpg" alt="Baquates" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dorieo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Latin_inscription_of_Volubilis_10.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> writers disagree on where the Baquates lived, some placing them in the far south of Mauretania Tingitana and others in the east. Ptolemy names them twice in his Geography, about 150, under two spellings. The Antonine Itinerary of the 3rd century puts them with the Makanitai in the south, as does one passage of Julius Honorius in the late 4th century, while another places them beyond the Moulouya river, next to the Bavares. Inscriptions put them near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volubilis">Volubilis</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-atlas">Middle Atlas</a>. They were probably a semi-nomadic mountain people who wintered in the valleys, and may have served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as federates far to the east, perhaps guarding the routes between the two Mauretanias. Roman lists call them barbarians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauri">Mauri</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1715">Fifteen</a> inscriptions name them. Under Hadrian, between 117 and 122, they raided Cartennas in Mauretania Caesariensis. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius">Antoninus Pius</a> their leader Tuccuda bore the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeps">princeps</a> and the Roman name Aelius, a sign of citizenship and client status. Allied with the Makanitai, probably before 168, they destroyed a garrison town near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volubilis">Volubilis</a>, which led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> to wall the city; their princeps then, Ucmet, was not a citizen. Peace was restored by 175 and renewed in 180, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> named a new citizen princeps and took his son hostage, but by 200 a new agreement was needed with a leader who was not a citizen.</p><p>The friction probably arose from the growth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volubilis">Volubilis</a> onto the Baquates' pastures. In 235 two legati were sent with troops against their alliance with the Bavares, and four meetings between 239 and 245 show hard bargaining. In the inscriptions of 277 and 280 their leader is called rex, king, and the peace perennial. When the Roman army left Volubilis in 284 the city was not attacked; the last pacts probably secured its inhabitants, and the Baquates likely regained their pastures.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baquates” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Berber tribes such as the <b>Baquates</b> negotiated treaties with Rome, recorded on altars at Volubilis.</blockquote><a href="/state/mauretania-tingitana">Mauretania Tingitana</a></li></ul></section>
